	abstract = {The success of first UK flight of Watchkeeper unmanned air vehicle (UAV) in Aberporth, west Wales, represents longer-term program to address key technological and regulatory issues, allowing UAVs to fly in civil airspace. Watchkeeper is based on the Hermes drone by Israeli-firm Elbit, which has notched up more than 30,000 flight hours in Afghanistan and Iraq since 2007. Watchkeeper's new I-Master radar is very sophisticated and is able to detect men walking on the ground from many kilometres away. Watchkeeper features an aerofoil shape that reduces ice accumulation with a novel electro-expulsive leading-edge system to remove ice, along with a fuel injection system that eliminates the need for a carburettor. The UAV will he powered by a Wankel rotary engine, made by UAV Engines in Lichfield, driving a two-bladed propeller. Watchkeeper will also undergo grass strip operation testing at ParcAberporth.},
