	abstract = {Ragweed (Ambrosia artemisiifolia L.) is an invasive plant rapidly spreading throughout Europe. Beside the yield losses in agricultural fields the plant causes allergic reactions of humans. To prevent further spreading the plant must be controlled in fields, ruderal areas, along railway lines and roads. To locate A. artemisiifolia patches reliable monitoring strategies have to be developed. Camera sensors attached to ground vehicles or drones have the potential to measure large areas. In September 2014 aerial and ground truth color images were taken within a harvested winter rye field with a high ragweed infestation. Afterwards the ragweed biomass within the image area of the ground truth images was determined. High variability of A. artemisiifolia in the RGB and HSV color space was obtained. The normalized grey values of ragweed, mugwort (Artemisia vulgaris L.) and winter rye (Secale cereale L.) plants were overlapping each other. The correlation of the classified coverage level in the 20 ground truth images using the same normalized color thresholds with the manual classified coverage level ("ground truth") as well as biomass was only weak. The correlation was improved by using variable color thresholds. The correlation was also weak between the coverage level of the aerial image, the “ground truth” coverage level and the biomass. © 2015, Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg.},
