	pages = {503 – 513},
	doi = {10.1080/00934690.2017.1383106},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85031810461&doi=10.1080%2f00934690.2017.1383106&partnerID=40&md5=b61334196c320c9a5e8f020ab401709e},
	abstract = {Grasslands pose a particular problem for archaeological survey involving artifact assessment, as no material is plowed up to the surface. Although finds in molehills are often the only source of information available on sites with such low visibility, an archaeology of molehills has thus far been largely disregarded. Yet, by applying a low-cost and time-efficient methodology, both the potential and the pitfalls of molehill archaeology come to the forefront. At the medieval harbor site of Monnikerede, it was possible to assess material culture and to locate certain structural elements. However, when artifact densities were compared with the underlying geophysical anomalies, a more complex relationship appeared at the level of individual features. © 2017, © Trustees of Boston University 2017.},
